WEBVTT
TITLE Tcp Retransmission Timeout And Rtt Estimation — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A TCP sender keeps one retransmission timer. Its duration is the RTO.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The timer runs for the oldest unacknowledged segment, and an ACK for new data restarts it.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
With a 1200 ms timer and ACKs at 400 and 800 ms, the deadline moves to 1600 then 2000 ms.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
On expiry the sender retransmits the earliest unacknowledged segment and doubles the timer.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The timer is a bet on how long an ACK can take. Both errors are paid in bandwidth.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
A 120 ms timer on a 400 ms path fires three times before the first ACK arrives.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
A 12 second timer on the same path waits thirty round trips before repairing one loss.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So the timer must adapt to the path, and the path changes minute by minute.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Before any measurement the timer is one second. RFC 6298 lowered this from three.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The first sample R sets the centre to R and the spread to R over 2.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
With R = 40 ms the rule gives 40 + 4 x 20 = 120 ms, then the floor raises it to 1000 ms.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The floor is deliberate: a minimum timer keeps TCP conservative on fast paths.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A new sample moves the centre by one eighth of the gap, and the spread by one quarter.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Five samples: 400, 380, 420, 400 and 800 ms, all computed on this page.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The centre ends at 450.24 ms and the spread at 168.54 ms, so the timer is 1124.42 ms.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Notice the outlier did not drag the centre all the way to 800; it moved the spread more.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The two lines of rule 2.3 must run in the order written: spread first.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
On the fifth sample the correct order gives RTTVAR 168.54 ms and RTO 1124.42 ms.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Swapping the order gives 156.05 ms and a timer of 1074.45 ms: about 50 ms short.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The error always points the same way: a gap measured from a centre that already moved.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A steady path converges the spread toward zero, so the timer sits just above the round trip.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
One sample of 1600 ms lifts the centre to 550 and the spread to 300.04 ms.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The timer grew by a factor of 1.75 from one sample, and it decays back by three quarters a sample.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
So the estimator is a low-pass filter, not a maximum: one delay does not set the timer forever.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
After a retransmission, an arriving ACK may answer either copy. That is the ambiguity.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
An ACK at 1250 ms after a retransmission at 1200 gives 50 ms for a 400 ms path.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The other scenario: the ACK for the copy arrives at 1600 ms, so timing from the original gives 1600.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Karn's rule: take no sample from a retransmitted segment; keep the timer and let it back off.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Every expiry doubles the timer: 1200, 2400, 4800 and on.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
From 1200 ms the ladder reaches the 60 second maximum after six doublings.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Backoff and recomputation are different operations, and only an ACK ends the backoff.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
One more rule: if the timer expires waiting for a SYN ACK, the RTO returns to 3 seconds for data.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
On a constant path the spread after n samples is 200 times 0.75 to the n minus 1.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The computed timer comes within ten per cent of the round trip at the twelfth sample.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The constants worth carrying: 1/8, 1/4, K = 4, a 1 s floor and a 60 s ceiling.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Every figure on this page is computed, and the count of passing checks is printed under the canvas.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: ask whether a sample exists, because that decides which rule applies.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: apply the floor and the ceiling after computing, never before.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: a retransmission in the history means no sample, and a doubling instead.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The four slips: a mean instead of a filter, a variance, a swapped order, and a clamped spread.

